## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to model interval scheduling with a cap on concurrent starts, a common real-world constraint in job scheduling and ad campaigns.

## Key Concepts
- Weighted interval scheduling with a limit on number of intervals
- Preprocessing next[i] via binary search
- DP with reconstruction pointers for actual selection

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode sketch for dp and reconstruction
function maxProfitTasks(s, d, p, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const idx = [...Array(n).keys()].sort((a,b)=> s[a]-s[b]);
  // reorder s,d,p by idx as needed; compute next[] with binary search
  const next = Array(n).fill(n);
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) {
    let limit = s[i] + d[i] - 1;
    // binary search for first j with s[j] > limit
    let lo=i+1, hi=n; while (lo<hi){
      let mid=(lo+hi)>>1; if (s[mid] > limit) hi=mid; else lo=mid+1; }
    next[i] = lo;
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(0));
  const take = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(false));
  for (let i=n-1;i>=0;i--) {
    for (let t=1;t<=k;t++) {
      if (dp[i+1][t] >= p[i] + dp[next[i]][t-1]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i+1][t];
        take[i][t] = false;
      } else {
        dp[i][t] = p[i] + dp[next[i]][t-1];
        take[i][t] = true;
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {profit: dp[0][k], take, next};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a fresh DP angle: interval-based value depends on distinct characters within each substring, plus precise reconstruction for k blocks.

## Key Concepts

- Non-overlapping interval DP
- Precomputing distinct counts for all substrings
- Reconstruction via back-pointers
- Time/space trade-offs and potential optimizations

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch
function maxKSubstrings(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dist = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    const freq = new Array(26).fill(0);
    let setBits = 0;
    for (let j = i; j >= 0; j--) {
      const idx = s.charCodeAt(j) - 97;
      if (freq[idx]++ === 0) setBits++;
      dist[j][i] = setBits;
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prev = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  const start = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    dp[i][0] = 0;
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      // Option 1: skip i
      if (dp[i-1][t] > dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
        prev[i][t] = i-1;
        start[i][t] = start[i-1][t];
      }

      // Option 2: end t-th substring at i
      for (let s = 1; s <= i; s++) {
        const val = dp[s-1][t-1] + dist[s-1][i-1];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          prev[i][t] = s-1;
          start[i][t] = s-1;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct substrings
  const result = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && i > 0) {
    if (prev[i][t] === i-1) {
      i--;
    } else {
      result.unshift([start[i][t]+1, i]);
      i = prev[i][t];
      t--;
    }
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][k], substrings: result };
}
```

Time complexity: O(n²k) for DP transitions + O(n²) for preprocessing. Space: O(n² + nk). Optimizations include limiting the inner loop or using convex hull tricks for special cases.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with a non-adjacent selection constraint and exact count, forcing clarity on state definition and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP on two dimensions (i, t) with take/skip choices. 
- Non-adjacency enforced by using i-2 when taking i. 
- Backtracking recovers chosen indices; space optimization possible.

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKNonAdjacent(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(false));
  for(let i=0;i<=n;i++) dp[i][0]=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      const val = (i>=2 ? dp[i-2][t-1] : (t===1 ? 0 : -Infinity)) + A[i-1];
      if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; take[i][t] = true; }
    }
  }
  let res = [], i=n, t=k;
  while(i>0 && t>0){
    if(take[i][t]){ res.push(i); i-=2; t--; } else { i--; }
  }
  res.reverse();
  return {sum: dp[n][k], indices: res};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for at-most k selections? 
- How would you optimize space to O(k)?

## Why This Question Is Asked

This problem tests dynamic programming with turn constraints on grid paths, requiring careful state design and path reconstruction. It evaluates understanding of edge case handling and performance optimization for large inputs.

## Key Concepts

- **4D DP state**: position (i, j), turn count (t), and ending direction (d)
- **Two transition types**: continuing in the same direction versus making a turn
- **Path reconstruction**: using parent pointers or backtracking to retrieve the actual path
- **Time and space trade-offs**: consider optimization for large grids

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPathWithKTurns(M, k) {
  const n = M.length;
  const m = M[0].length;
  const dp = Array(n).fill(null).map(() =>
    Array(m).fill(null).map(() =>
      Array(k + 1).fill(null).map(() => [-1, -1])
    )
  );

  dp[0][0][0][0] = dp[0][0][0][1] = M[0][0];

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        for (let d = 0; d < 2; d++) {
          if (dp[i][j][t][d] < 0) continue;

          if (i + 1 < n) {
            const newD = 1;
            const newT = d === newD ? t : t + 1;
            if (newT <= k) {
              dp[i + 1][j][newT][newD] = Math.max(
                dp[i + 1][j][newT][newD],
                dp[i][j][t][d] + M[i + 1][j]
              );
            }
          }

          if (j + 1 < m) {
            const newD = 0;
            const newT = d === newD ? t : t + 1;
            if (newT <= k) {
              dp[i][j + 1][newT][newD] = Math.max(
                dp[i][j + 1][newT][newD],
                dp[i][j][t][d] + M[i][j + 1]
              );
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  return Math.max(dp[n - 1][m - 1][k][0], dp[n - 1][m - 1][k][1]);
}
```

## Complexity

- **Time**: O(n × m × k)
- **Space**: O(n × m × k)

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP on ordered subsequences with reconstruction, a common interview pattern that scales gracefully from beginner to moderate.

## Key Concepts
- State dp[i][t] represents best sum ending at i as the t-th pick
- Transition uses max over previous indices; reconstruction via parent pointers
- Space/time trade-offs; potential O(nk) optimization via running maxima

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxDotSubsequence(A,B,k){
  const n=A.length;
  const dp= Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par= Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){ dp[i][1] = A[i]*B[i]; par[i][1] = -1; }
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
      let best=-Infinity; let arg=-1;
      for(let j=0;j<i;j++){
        if(dp[j][t-1] > best){ best=dp[j][t-1]; arg=j; }
      }
      if(best>-Infinity){
        dp[i][t] = best + A[i]*B[i];
        par[i][t] = arg;
      }
    }
  }
  let bestEnd=-1, bestVal=-Infinity;
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) if(dp[i][k] > bestVal){ bestVal=dp[i][k]; bestEnd=i; }
  const indices=[]; let i=bestEnd, t=k;
  while(i>=0 && t>=1){ indices.push(i); i=par[i][t]; t--; }
  indices.reverse();
  return {value: bestVal, indices};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) time and O(nk) space using prefix maxima per t-1?
- How would you extend to handle k up to n with space-saving tricks?

